<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/advanced/resource-provision-food-energy">
  <h2>How food is produced</h2>
  <p>
    Farming types (syllabus 10.1.1) run on one axis: who eats the output.
    <strong>Subsistence</strong> farming feeds the family first — monsoon rice paddies in
    Bangladesh; <strong>commercial</strong> farming sells into markets — Kansas wheat or
    Brazilian soy. Within that, <strong>arable</strong> (crops), <strong>pastoral</strong>
    (animals) and <strong>mixed</strong> describe the product; and at the high-tech edge,
    <strong>hydroponics, aeroponics and aquaponics</strong> grow food without soil at all —
    the Netherlands, smaller than Scotland, is the world's second-largest agricultural
    exporter by value. Every farm is a <strong>system</strong> (10.1.2): inputs (labour,
    seed, water, fertiliser, machinery), processes (ploughing, sowing, irrigating,
    harvesting), outputs (grain, milk, meat — or little surplus at all, which is the
    subsistence trap).
  </p>

  <h2>Enough food, and not enough</h2>
  <p>
    Global patterns (10.2.1) are stark: North America and Europe eat roughly 3,400–3,700
    kcal per person per day; Sub-Saharan Africa averages around 2,400–2,500. Yet the world
    grows enough for everyone — the calculator below shows it. Global cereal output is about
    2.8 Gt a year from ~710 million hectares at ~4 t/ha (FAO figures, rounded), which is
    ~355 kg of cereal per person per year — over 3,000 kcal a day <em>if it reached a human
    mouth directly</em> instead of animal feed, fuel tanks and landfills. It doesn't: FAO's
    2023 report counts ~735 million undernourished people and ~2.4 billion moderately or
    severely food insecure. The geographer's insight, from economist Amartya Sen, is that
    most hunger is a failure of <em>access</em> (poverty, conflict, distribution), not of
    global supply — roughly a third of food is lost or wasted between field and plate.
    Strategies to raise supply (10.2.3, 10.3.3) include irrigation, better storage, new seed
    varieties and the Great Green Wall against Sahelian desertification; food aid (10.3.4)
    saves lives in famine — Ethiopia 1984–85 — but can undercut local farmers when free
    grain floods the same market. Globalisation of food (10.2.4, 10.2.5) delivers strawberries
    in December, but transmits shocks: when war hit the Black Sea breadbasket in 2022, prices
    jumped in Cairo and Nairobi.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the world's calorie budget: drag population and yields — supply per person stays comfortable on paper, which is exactly why hunger is a distribution problem" widget="sliderLab" config={foodCfg} />

  <h2>Energy: the global mix in motion</h2>
  <p>
    Energy types (10.4.1) split into <strong>renewables</strong> (biomass, geothermal,
    hydroelectric, solar, tidal, wave, wind), <strong>non-renewables</strong> (coal, oil,
    gas, nuclear), and <strong>fuelwood</strong> — renewable only if regrown. Demand keeps
    rising (10.5.1) with population and industrialisation, but the supply mix is shifting
    unevenly: fig. 2 shows electricity, the fastest-moving sector. Coal-plus-gas slid from
    ~62% to ~58% of world generation between 2010 and 2023, hydro holds near 14–16%, nuclear
    sagged after Fukushima, and wind plus solar climbed from ~2% to over 13% (Ember, rounded).
    Where surpluses and deficits lie (10.5.2) is mostly geology and luck: the Gulf sits on a
    third of the world's oil; Japan imports nearly all its energy, so <strong>energy
    security</strong> — reliable, affordable supply — is existential there. Before 2022,
    Russia supplied around 40% of Europe's imported gas; the war showed what dependence
    costs. Mixes also differ by development level (10.5.3): firewood still dominates household
    energy in much of Sub-Saharan Africa, while Iceland runs on hydro and geothermal and
    HICs burn money-efficient mixes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="world electricity by source, 2010 to 2023 (shares in %, rounded, Ember) — watch solar's slope against the fossil plateau" widget="plotter" config={energyCfg} />

  <h2>The price of power — and managing it</h2>
  <p>
    Every source has a bill (10.6.1). Counted over its whole life, coal emits ~820 g of CO₂
    per kWh, gas ~490, solar ~45 and wind ~11 (IPCC medians). Land matters too: nuclear is
    compact but leaves waste for millennia; wind farms occupy wide areas yet the farming goes
    on between turbines; dams flood valleys — China's Three Gorges displaced over a million
    people — and reservoirs emit methane. Extraction scars: mountaintop coal, oil spills
    (Exxon Valdez 1989, Deepwater Horizon 2010), and around 40% of the US corn crop going
    into ethanol, competing with plates. The named example (10.6.4) is <strong>Brazil</strong>:
    its electricity is about 60% hydro, transport runs heavily on sugarcane ethanol (~30
    billion litres a year, a programme begun with Proálcool in 1975), and most new cars are
    flex-fuel — yet Brazil is also a major offshore oil exporter, and dams and ethanol push
    against the rainforest. Managing supplies sustainably (10.6.2, 10.6.3) means stacking
    strategies: efficiency first (cheapest barrel is the one never burned), diversified
    renewables for security, storage and grids to firm them up, and honest pricing of the
    emissions. The 1973 shock taught the lesson once; the 2022 spike set it as homework.
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="food and energy are one story of provision: blight to breadbasket war, embargo to renewables — the shocks that rewrote supply" widget="timeline" config={resTimeline} />

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Geography 0460 syllabus (2027–2029), topic 10; FAO, The State of Food Security and Nutrition in the World 2023 and FAOSTAT; Ember Global Electricity Review 2024; IPCC lifecycle emissions medians (AR5 Annex III); EPE/UNICA Brazil energy data.</p>
</TopicPage>
